  "answer_text": "# \ud83e\uddb4 Calcium-Containing Foods\n\nCalcium is the most abundant mineral in your body and a major component of your bones and teeth. Since your body can't make calcium on its own, it's essential to consume calcium-rich foods to meet your daily needs.\n\nCalcium is best known for building bones and keeping them healthy, but it also plays many other vital roles \u2014 like keeping your heart beating, muscles and nerves functioning, and helping maintain healthy blood pressure.\n\n---\n\n## \ud83e\udd5b Dairy Products\nWhen you think of high-calcium foods, dairy products like **milk, yogurt, and cheese** probably come to mind first. Indeed, dairy foods are some of nature's most concentrated sources of calcium, and many come packaged with other bone-building nutrients like protein, phosphorus, and (when fortified) vitamin D.\n\n- \ud83e\uddc0 **Swiss cheese** \u2013 ~1,175 mg per cup (diced)\n- \ud83e\udd5b **Milk** (whole, skim, or low-fat)\n- \ud83c\udf66 **Yogurt** (plain or fruit, low-fat) \u2013 ~206\u2013235 mg per 6 oz container\n- \ud83e\uddc0 **Hard cheese** \u2013 A 3 cm cube of hard cheese has as much calcium as a cup of milk.\n\n---\n\n## \ud83c\udf3f Plant-Based Sources\nMost vegetables, legumes, nuts, seeds, and dried fruit contain some calcium.\n\n- \ud83e\udd66 **Broccoli**\n- \ud83e\udd6c **Kale, collard greens, bok choy**\n- \ud83e\uded8 **Beans** (black beans, chickpeas, white beans)\n- \ud83c\udf30 **Almonds**\n- \ud83e\udd57 **Tofu** (if processed with calcium sulfate)\n- \ud83c\udf3e **Turnip greens** \u2013 ~200+ mg per cooked cup\n\nNote that some plant foods contain compounds like insoluble fiber and oxalic acid that can bind with calcium and hinder its absorption. For example, greens like spinach and chard are higher in oxalic acid \u2014 but kale, collard greens, and bok choy contain lower amounts, so their calcium is likely better absorbed.\n\n---\n\n## \ud83d\udc1f Fish & Seafood\n- \ud83d\udc1f **Canned salmon** \u2013 ~241 mg per 3 oz\n- \ud83d\udc1f **Canned sardines** \u2013 Canned salmon or sardines are excellent sources of calcium when you eat the softened bones from the canning process.\n\n---\n\n## \ud83e\udd64 Fortified Foods & Beverages\nCalcium-fortified soy, almond, and rice beverages, as well as calcium-fortified orange juice, are good non-dairy options (check the nutrition labels).\n\n- Fortified cereals\n- Fortified breads\n- Fortified plant-based milks (soy, almond, oat, rice)\n\n---\n\n## \ud83d\udcca Quick Summary Table\n\n| Food | Calcium (approx.) |\n|---|---|\n| Swiss cheese (1 cup diced) | 1,175 mg |\n| Milk (1 cup) | ~300 mg |\n| Yogurt (6 oz) | ~200\u2013235 mg |\n| Canned salmon (3 oz) | ~241 mg |\n| Black beans (1 cup raw) | ~239 mg |\n| Turnip greens (1 cup cooked) | ~200 mg |\n| Almonds | ~75 mg per oz |\n| Broccoli (1 cup cooked) | ~60 mg |\n\n---\n\n## \u26a0\ufe0f Tips to Remember\n- Calcium-rich foods and calcium-fortified foods are the preferred choices for obtaining calcium because additional nutrients like vitamin D in milk can contribute to bone development and the prevention of osteoporosis.\n- Even individuals who are lactose intolerant are usually able to eat small amounts of dairy products such as yogurt, cheese, and lactase-treated milk, especially when eaten as part of a meal.\n- The calcium content listed for most foods is estimated and can vary due to multiple factors \u2014 check the food label to determine how much calcium is in a particular product.",
